  "step_3": "The decision reason prefers the more right-skewed lottery. Lottery A is more right-skewed (100% on unfavorable) than Lottery B (99.9% on neutral, 0.1% on unfavorable). The individual preferred Lottery A. However, their justification was based on risk aversion, the smallness of the certain loss, the unattractiveness of a large potential loss even with low probability, and the perceived imbalance between risk and reward—especially the idea that a 50% chance of a big loss would be unattractive (a heuristic, not a skewness-based argument). They did not mention skewness, probability distribution shape, or the relative concentration of probability on unfavorable outcomes. Their reasoning focused on magnitude of loss, probability of loss, and intuitive risk perception, not on the statistical property of skewness. Therefore, although the decision aligns with the reason’s preference (A over B), the reasoning process does not reflect the use of the skewness criterion. The individual did not consider or refer to the skewness of the lotteries. Thus, the reason was not used in the deliberation process.",
  "final_assessment_score": 20
